  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nCloud cover has decreased this afternoon following a line of \nsprinkles that propagated through the area this morning ahead of a \ncold front. This cold front, and associated 500mb trough, are \ncurrently moving through the CWA this afternoon, with cooler and \ndrier air from Canada already starting to filter in. Skies are \nexpected to remain clear through tonight as high pressure builds \nover the Upper Great Lakes region. With low temps forecast to drop \ninto the 30s tonight for most, this will likely lead to areas of \nfog. Patchy dense fog may also be possible, especially in northwest \nWI where the drier airmass will be arriving latest. \n\nThursday will see a continuation of the benign weather pattern that \nhas dominated this past week as ridging aloft and sfc high pressure \npersists. Despite the ongoing cold fropa, high temps tomorrow will \nbe unseasonably warm on Thursday in the mid 60s to low 70s. The \nexception will be locations near the North Shore, as northeast flow \nover the lake and southeast flow inland will advect cooler air from \nLake Superior onshore. \n\nA change in the dry pattern we've been seeing arrives on Friday as \nisentropic uplift associated with a warm frontal passage brings a \nchance for showers and potentially a rumble of thunder. Highest \nchances (50-65%) for rainfall will be in north-central MN, with the \nprobability decreasing for locations farther to the east. \n\nAdditional rain chances begin to spread across the CWA this weekend \nas the low pressure associated with Friday's warm frontal passage \ncontinues to propagate north into northern Saskatchewan and Manitoba \nand a strong baroclinic zone becomes draped over the Northland. The \n500mb trough that will be proving dynamic forcing to this low \npressure is forecast to begin propagating downstream on Sunday, \nbreaking down the omega block that has been keeping the recent \npattern quiet. \n\nAn active synoptic pattern is favored to develop early next week \nwith split flow aloft and additional troughs and areas of sfc low \npressure move into the Upper Midwest. Whether or not these lows \nbring precipitation to the CWA remains uncertain, as they will \nbe needing to overcome areas of high pressure and drier air, \nespecially in the Borderlands, northern Ontario, and southern \nManitoba. One of the biggest changes will be cooler \ntemperatures. Highs on Monday and Tuesday are currently forecast\nto be in the mid 40s to low 50s with overnight lows dipping \nbelow freezing for a majority of the CWA.",
